We have template 1679747. Need to propose retrosynthesis route for target SMILES: C=C(C1=CC=C(OC)C=C1)C2=CC=C(OC)C=C2. That's a stilbene derivative with two anisole (methoxy phenyl) groups attached to a central double bond (trans? but not needed). The template appears to be a retro reaction that breaks a double bond and forms a Si-CH2-Li and a carbonyl? Let's examine the retro_smarts_template: '[CH2;D1;+0:1]=[C;H0;D3;+0:2](-[c:3])-[c:4]>>C-[Si](-C)(-C)-[CH2;D2;+0:1]-[Li].O=[C;H0;D3;+0:2](-[c:3])-[c:4]'

So forward reaction (retro template) is: product (target) is a alkene with CH2= C (substituted) attached to aromatic rings. The retrosynthetic direction: break the alkene into a silyl carbanion (C-[Si](C)(C)-CH2-Li) and a carbonyl (O=C- aromatic). So the precursors are a silyl reagent (maybe Me3SiCH2Li?) and an aromatic aldehyde? Actually O=[C;H0;D3;+0:2](-[c:3])-[c:4] is a carbonyl attached to aromatic (maybe a benzaldehyde). So the template suggests disconnection of the alkene into a silyl carbanion and an aldehyde (or ketone). The product is a substituted alkene formed by addition of the silyl carbanion to the carbonyl? Wait forward reaction: C-[Si](-C)(-C)-[CH2;D2;+0:1]-[Li] + O=[C;H0;D3;+0:2](-[c:3])-[c:4] -> [CH2;D1;+0:1]=[C;H0;D3;+0:2](-[c:3])-[c:4]. So it's a nucleophilic addition of a silyl carbanion to a carbonyl, then elimination of Li? Actually forms an alkene via a Wittig-like? Might be a Peterson olefination: silyl carbanion (silyl carbanion) reacts with carbonyl to give β-hydroxysilane then elimination to alkene. So the template is a Peterson olefination.

Thus retrosynthesis: target alkene can be made from corresponding carbonyl (aryl aldehyde) and a silyl carbanion (e.g., (trimethylsilyl)methyl lithium). For our target, we have two aromatic rings each with OMe substituent. The carbonyl would be a benzaldehyde with OMe (anisaldehyde). The product has two aromatic rings attached to the double bond: one side is CH2= C(Ar1)-Ar2? Actually target: C=C(C1=CC=C(OC)C=C1)C2=CC=C(OC)C=C2. This is trans-stilbene with both phenyl rings methoxy-substituted at para? The SMILES: C=C(C1=CC=C(OC)C=C1)C2=CC=C(OC)C=C2. The central double bond: C=C(Ar1)Ar2. So one carbon of double bond is CH (attached to Ar1) and the other carbon is attached to Ar2 and also a CH? Actually the SMILES: C=C(Ar1)Ar2 means the central carbon (C) is attached to Ar1 as substituent, and the other carbon is attached to Ar2. The double bond is between a terminal CH2? Wait "C=C(" indicates first carbon is CH2? Actually "C=" first carbon is sp2 carbon with two hydrogens? The SMILES "C=C(" means CH2= C(...). So the left side is CH2, right side is carbon attached to two aryl groups? Actually "C=C(C1=... )C2=..." means CH2= C(Ar1) - C2=... So the double bond is between CH2 and a carbon bearing Ar1 and attached to Ar2 via single bond? Wait there is "C=C(C1=... )C2=..." So after the double bond, the carbon is attached to Ar1 as a substituent and also attached to C2=... which is aromatic ring attached via a single bond? Actually "C2=CC=..." indicates that carbon is part of aromatic ring? The SMILES "C2=CC=..." indicates a phenyl attached directly to the double bond carbon (i.e., stilbene). So the double bond carbon is sp2 attached to phenyl ring (C2=...). So the product is CH2= C(Ar1)-Ar2 (stilbene). So the carbonyl precursor would be Ar2-CHO (anisaldehyde) and the silyl carbanion would be CH2-SiMe3? Actually the carbonyl carbon becomes the carbon of the double bond attached to Ar2. The CH2 side comes from the silyl carbanion CH2-SiMe3 (or CH2-SiR3). So the retrosynthesis: target -> reaction using template 1679747 -> precursors: (trimethylsilyl)methyl lithium (or its equivalent) and anisaldehyde (para-methoxybenzaldehyde). But we also need the other aryl group attached to the carbon bearing the CH2? Wait the carbonyl is attached to Ar2 (anisaldehyde). The other aryl group (Ar1) is attached to the same carbon as the carbonyl? In the product, the carbon attached to CH2 also has Ar1 as substituent. In the carbonyl precursor, the carbonyl carbon is attached to Ar2 only, not Ar1. So how does Ar1 get attached? The template shows the carbonyl carbon attached to two aromatic groups? Actually O=[C;H0;D3;+0:2](-[c:3])-[c:4] indicates carbonyl carbon attached to two aromatic carbons (c:3 and c:4). That suggests a diaryl ketone carbonyl (like benzophenone). Indeed carbonyl attached to two aryl groups (a diaryl ketone). So the carbonyl precursor is a diaryl ketone (benzophenone derivative) where both aryl groups are present. Then the Peterson olefination yields a stilbene with CH2 attached to one side? Wait product is CH2= C(Ar1)-Ar2? The carbonyl carbon attached to two aryl groups becomes the alkene carbon attached to both aryl groups. So the carbonyl is a diaryl ketone (Ar-CO-Ar). The silyl carbanion provides CH2 group. So the product is CH2= C(Ar1)-Ar2 (stilbene). Yes.

Thus our target is a diaryl alkene with both aryl groups methoxy-substituted. So the carbonyl precursor is the corresponding diaryl ketone: O=C(C1=CC=C(OC)C=C1)C2=CC=C(OC)C=C2 (i.e., bis(4-methoxyphenyl) ketone). That's 4,4'-dimethoxybenzophenone. The silyl carbanion is (trimethylsilyl)methyl lithium (or its equivalent). So the retrosynthesis route: target -> reaction (template 1679747) -> children: precursor1: "C[Si](C)(C)C"?? Actually the silyl carbanion is "C[Si](C)(C)[Li]"? The template expects "C-[Si](-C)(-C)-[CH2;D2;+0:1]-[Li]" which is a silyl group attached to CH2-Li. So the SMILES for that reagent could be "C[Si](C)(C)C[Li]"? Not exactly. Might be "C[Si](C)(C)C[Li]" but that is not standard. Usually the reagent is "C[Si](C)(C)C[Li]" representing (trimethylsilyl)methyl lithium. However we can just provide a placeholder SMILES for the silyl carbanion: "C[Si](C)(C)C[Li]" (though Li attached to carbon). Might be "C[Si](C)(C)C[Li]" is okay.

The other precursor is the diaryl ketone: "O=C(C1=CC=C(OC)C=C1)C2=CC=C(OC)C=C2". That's the SMILES for 4,4'-dimethoxybenzophenone.

